eyes_from:
RenderEye(o, 22, 23) out of ZzzCharacter.cpp, called from RenderCharacter's model switch on exactly one condition:

    case MODEL_BULL_FIGHTER:
    case MODEL_DEATH_COW:
        if ((o->Type == MODEL_BULL_FIGHTER && c->Level == 1) || (o->Type == MODEL_DEATH_COW))
            RenderEye(o, 22, 23);

c->Level == 1 is how the server's Elite is marked - CreateMonster sets it in this variant's branch and nowhere else on this model - so the eyes are the flag, not a decoration on it. And RenderEye itself is four lines:

    float Luminosity = sinf(WorldTime * 0.002f) * 0.3f + 0.8f;
    Vector(Luminosity, Luminosity, Luminosity, Light);
    Vector(5.f, 0.f, 0.f, p);
    b->TransformPosition(o->BoneTransform[Left], p, Position, true);
    CreateSprite(BITMAP_SHINY + 3, Position, fSize, Light, NULL);
    Vector(-5.f, 0.f, 0.f, p);   // and again on Right

Five units out along each eye bone's own X, in opposite directions, which widens the pair; the sheet is Effect/eye01.jpg at 32x16; the light is grey and pulses between 0.5 and 1.1 on a 3.1-second period off the world clock. Every number here is that call and nothing has been invented.

**Not a tint.** This is the finding, and it is worth writing down because it is the obvious guess and it is wrong: nothing about the Elite is recoloured. Not the body, not the sheets, not the light the model is drawn under. The red is in eye01.jpg - an almond of red on black, chroma 87 against under one in the other two channels - and the model beneath it is the plain Bull Fighter's own art. LEGACY's own note reached the same conclusion and said so: 'a pair of glowing eyes, switched on by c->Level == 1 - not a tint, which is the obvious guess and wrong.'

Offsets are MU's, in the bone's own axes, in MU units - the same convention the snort's [0, -4, 0] uses and the same one the exporter preserves, because a bone's rest rotation crosses into the .glb unchanged. See client/core/Eyes.cs.
